WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.249
Hardly any topic stirs people up as reliably as the supposed decline of their own language. Letters to the editor lament the 'Denglisch' in advertising, parents worry about their children's spelling, and on social media people fight over gender-inclusive language as if it were a matter of life and death. The diagnosis seems clear: German is going to the dogs.

00:00:23.499 --> 00:00:56.904
Linguists generally react calmly to such complaints. They point out that languages have always changed and that every generation has lamented the decline of the language. As early as the seventeenth century, language societies sprang up to protect German from the overwhelming influence of French. Words like 'Balkon', 'Friseur' or 'Portemonnaie', once regarded as foreign bodies, now strike hardly anyone as foreign.

00:00:57.154 --> 00:01:32.478
The dispute over the 1996 spelling reform was just as fierce. Newspapers announced they would not adopt the new rules, writers spoke of a cultural rupture, and opponents took the matter all the way to the Federal Constitutional Court. A generation later, schoolchildren write 'dass' with a double s without giving it a thought, and the old spelling 'daß' looks as old-fashioned to them as a sign in blackletter. What once counted as destruction is simply the norm to them.

00:01:32.728 --> 00:02:03.786
That does not mean that all changes are unimportant. Anyone who cannot write a comprehensible email at work really is at a disadvantage, and when public authorities communicate in a language that only insiders understand, they shut people out. But these problems have little to do with anglicisms or youth slang. They concern whether we command different registers: whether we know how to write to a friend and how to write to an insurance company.

00:02:04.036 --> 00:02:30.055
Language decline, one might say pointedly, is usually just another word for language change that one has not gone along with oneself. The real challenge is not to protect German from foreign influence, but to give as many people as possible a confident command of its many varieties — from a chat message to a job application.
